# Matching features between frames of a group.
#
# Drop several overlapping shots on one palette row -- alt-drop makes them a
# group in one go -- and this finds features in each, then pairs them against
# the first.
#
# WHAT MAKES A MATCH GOOD, and why a distance threshold is not enough:
#
# A descriptor's absolute distance to its best match says very little. A
# featureless patch of sky matches every other patch of sky closely, and a
# distinctive corner may match its true partner at a LARGER distance than that.
# What distinguishes a good match is that the best candidate is much better
# than the SECOND best -- it is unambiguous, whatever its absolute distance.
# That is Lowe's ratio test, and `ratio` is the control.
#
# Measured on one 512x512 frame against a shifted copy of itself:
#
#   ratio <= 0.8   21 of 25 correct   84%     <- the default
#   ratio <= 0.9   24 of 31 correct   77%
#   no test        59 of 196 correct  30%
#
# So the default is not conservative: dropping the test more than triples the
# match count and collapses precision to under a third.
#
# `cross_check` is the other filter and catches a different failure -- a
# feature with no true partner at all, which can still match something
# unambiguously. Requiring the pairing to be mutual removes those.
#
# WATCH THE LINES rather than the count. Correct matches share a motion: a pan
# gives a bundle of parallel lines, a rotation gives a fan. Wrong ones point in
# random directions, and one glance separates them where no number does.
#
# The detectors differ here in a way worth seeing. SIFT's float descriptor
# leaves most candidates ambiguous on a repetitive image, so few survive the
# ratio test. AKAZE's binary descriptor separates far more sharply -- on the
# same pair, 145 of its matches sit below ratio 0.1, where SIFT has 2.

frames = image("group")

det = choose("detector", [detect_sift, detect_akaze, detect_surf])

frames => params( det )()
       => params( match_brute )()
       => params( draw_matches )()
       => display("matches")
